  <content>
  <section><para>In this lesson, you will learn how a function can be represented by two different equations. You will find any term of an arithmetic sequence directly, by using the nth term formula to find any term number \(n\).</para>
<para>When you finish this lesson, you will be able to:</para>
<list>
  <item>Describe the nth term.</item>
  <item>Find terms of sequences directly.</item>
  <item>Explain why different equations can represent the same sequence.</item>
</list>
<para>Here are the <emphasis>activities</emphasis> that will help you reach those goals:</para>
<list>
  <item>
    4.18.1 Repeated Operations
  </item>
  <item>
    4.18.2 Identifying Domain for a Function
  </item>
  <item>
    4.18.3 Define an Arithmetic Sequence by the nth Term
    <list>
      <item>
        4.18.3: Self Check
      </item>
      <item>
        4.18.3: Additional Resources
      </item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item>
    4.18.4 Define a Geometric Sequence by the nth Term
    <list>
      <item>
        4.18.4: Self Check
      </item>
      <item>
        4.18.4: Additional Resources
      </item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item>
    4.18.5 Interchanging between Formulas
    <list>
      <item>
        4.18.5: Self Check
      </item>
      <item>
        4.18.5: Additional Resources
      </item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item>
    4.18.6 Different Types of Equations
  </item>
</list>
<para>After that, you'll <emphasis>practice and review</emphasis>.</para>
<list>
  <item>
    4.18.7 Practice
  </item>
  <item>
    4.18.8 Lesson Summary
  </item>
</list>
</section>
